{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS.\nIf we discover how to connect quantum mechanics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with general relativity we’ll pretty much win \nphysics. There are multiple theories that claim  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10800,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to do this, but it’s notoriously difficult \nto test them. They seem to require absurd  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15800,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments like a particle collider the size \nof a galaxy. Or we could try to physics smarter,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23400,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead of physicsing harder. Let’s talk about \nsome ideas for quantum gravity experiments that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30160,
    "dDurationMs": 14280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be done on a non-galaxy-sized lab bench, \nand in some cases already have been done."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s been almost right on 100 years since quantum \nmechanics was discovered, and 110 since general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49720,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. Together these theories explain almost \neverything—quantum gives us the behaviour of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54960,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subatomic world and so the building blocks of \nall matter and their interactions via the quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces, and GR gives us the the background in \nwhich that matter lives—space and time along with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65320,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the force of gravity. Despite their incredible \nsuccesses in their own domains, the two seem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to contradict each other in deep ways. There \nmust be some master theory that encapsulates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both and resolves these contradictions, but \nin the century following the discoveries of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81880,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity and quantum mechanics, our \nefforts to find that theory have come up short."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87960,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The common approach to reconciling general \nrelativity with quantum mechanics has been  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91600,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to quantize gravity—to make the gravitational \nfield and so the geometry of spacetime obey the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97880,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strange rules of quantum mechanics. And why not? \nIt’s worked for everything else; for example we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made the electromagnetic field “quantum”, \nshowing it was mediated by force-carrying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon—and that was a stunning success. But \nquantizing gravity proved extraordinarily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difficult. The hypothetical force-carrier for \ngravity—the graviton—has an energy so tiny that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s hard to even conceive of an experiment that \ncould detect it directly. And then there’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact that the field of gravity is the fabric \nof spacetime itself. We quantized the other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135240,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forces of nature with respect to a continuous \nbackground, but so far it’s been exceptionally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "challenging to quantize that background itself. \nThe most prominent approaches, like string theory  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148600,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and loop quantum gravity, may have made progress, \nbut they struggle to make testable predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155880,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what if we’ve been looking at it the wrong way \naround. What if, instead of quantizing gravity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we should be trying to “gravitize the quantum”. \nThat is, instead of trying to give quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166600,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "properties to gravity, we should accept that \ngravity is fundamentally classical and try  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171160,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to understand how truly quantum matter \ncan result in truly classical gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we talked about approaches in which \ngravity stays classical in the last episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184120,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we explored the new Postquantum gravity \nhypothesis by Jonathan Oppenheim, in which random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuations in the gravitational field act on \nthe quantum wavefunction, causing it to collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We also talked about the Diose-Penrose model \na while ago, in which a superposition of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "different spatial distributions mass and \nenergy is ultimately forced to choose one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "such distribution consistent with \na singular gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211080,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are compelling reasons on \nboth sides of the discussion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but which is it? Is gravity quantized or is the \nquantum gravitized? Unlike many questions about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221480,
    "dDurationMs": 8440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum gravity, this one may be possible \nto answer with actual doable experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229920,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s consider the two scenarios and see what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232120,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions they make that \nwe might be able to test."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235920,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One: Gravity is quantum - this means gravity and \nby extension the fabric of spacetime exhibit the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242800,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "various weird properties of quantum world, \nlike superposition and entanglement. If we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247840,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can experimentally demonstrate one of these \nthings then we know gravity is quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253520,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two: The quantum is gravitized - this can be \ntested in the context of specific scenarios  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like the Diosi-Penrose theory or Oppenheim’s \nPostquantum gravity. I’m going to talk about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this first because it’s shorter, then I’ll \nget back to tests of actual quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In both the Diosi-Penrose and Oppenheim \napproaches, the gravitational field is responsible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for collapsing the quantum wavefunction. In these \nideas, there’s only one gravitational field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only one spacetime, and quantum systems do \nquantum stuff within that classical background.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the things quantum systems can do is \nbe in superpositions of states—they can exist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simultaneously in multiple states at once—like \nSchrodinger’s alive-and-dead cat. The job of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum wavefunction is to describe this range \nof possible states. Measurement or observation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“collapses the wavefunction” - it forces the \nsystem to choose one state from the possibilities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314080,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If gravity is classical, then the superposition \nof its contents shouldn’t cause a superposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the gravitational field. A superposition \nmight involve different distributions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass—which normally would mean different spacetime \ncurvatures—perhaps a superposition of spacetimes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330800,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if gravity is classical then there’s only one \nspacetime regardless of its contents. This could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336280,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lead to a tension between the gravitational \nfield and the matter producing that field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Diosi-Penrose approach says that \nwhen this tension gets too large,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction collapses. Another way \nto think about it is that the fuzziness  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352560,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the matter distribution is within a certain \nrange of matching a single spacetime curvature  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360240,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in that case it can stay in superposition. But \nif the distribution of matter strays outside that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367320,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range then the superposition collapses \ninto a well-defined matter distribution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374640,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oppenheim’s postquantum \ngravity is slightly different:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it states that the gravitational field \ncontains random fluctuations—called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational diffusion—that mess up quantum \nsuperpositions, causing them to collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both the Oppenheim and Diosi and \nPenrose make a similar prediction:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392000,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there should be a stochastic quality \nto the gravitational force or its effect.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gravitational diffusion might be the most \nstraightforward thing to test if it exists. It  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403560,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would imply that there’s a fundamental limit \nto the precision with which any mass can be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407680,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measured. So if we can build a set of scales \nwith a certain very high precision, and if the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413400,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weights it measures appear to fluctuate outside \nthat precision, it may suggest an intrinsic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty in the gravitational field. The most \naccurate experiment measuring a mass conducted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424960,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so far has led researchers to rule out versions \nof the theory with rapid spacetime diffusion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432080,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "implying that any gravity-induced collapse from \nthis mechanism is very weak. Oppenheim and team  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "propose other tabletop experiments that \ncould constrain this better in the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445160,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather than look for the funky \nbehavior of the gravitational field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448120,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can also approach this from the other \nside—by trying to see when the collapse of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452320,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction happens. If we can see where \nquantum superposition gives way to classical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458600,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter we may be able to figure out \nif it's gravity causing that collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465160,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both the Oppenheim and Diosi-Penrose models, \nas well as any of the family of models known  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470240,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as objective collapse theories predict that \nwavefunction collapse becomes increasingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely as an object grows in size or mass. If \nany of these ideas are correct, there should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481640,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a limit to how large an object we can put into \nsuperposition for any appreciable amount of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488760,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far we haven’t found such a hard limit, \nbut we’ve been able to put some pretty big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things in superposition—for example, getting \ninterference patterns from large molecules in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "double-slit experiment. Based on the largest \nsystems that have been put in superposition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504440,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can rule theories where the \ngravity-induced collapse is very strong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509200,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we have some constraints on these ideas \nwhere a classical gravity is influencing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum wavefunction of matter, and \nmore advanced tests are being planned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that may turn up something. Of course, it \nmay be that gravity really isn’t classical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the way these theories propose. It may \nbe that gravity really is quite quantum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just like matter. If that’s the case then,\nwe’ll need different experiments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537120,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two of the strangest properties of quantum \nobjects are superposition—which we just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talked a lot about—and entanglement. \nQuantum entanglement is when you have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two quantum systems that each may \nbe in a superposition of states,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the systems are also correlated so that the \nstate of one depends on the state of the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556840,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if a “quantum coin” was in \na superposition of both heads and tails,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562640,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while a pair of quantum coins could be both in \nsuperposition, but also be entangled so be in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the opposite state to each other. Even though \neach of the coins is fundamentally undefined in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its state. If the first coin is measured so that \nit’s wavefunction collapses to either heads or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tails, its entangled partner’s wavefunction \nwill immediately collapse to the opposite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588440,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum entanglement sounds like an exotic \nphenomenon, but it’s not; quantum particles are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593360,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constantly getting entangled with each other. \nIn fact, every interaction between particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generates entanglement, even if it doesn’t last \nvery long. We’ve observed these entanglements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when the interaction happens via the quantum \nforces—for example, when particles interact by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exchanging the photons of the electromagnetic \nforce. So what if we could cause particles to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615400,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "become entangled by a gravitational interaction? \nAs I’ll try to convince you, this would mean  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the gravitational field itself has to be \npart of the entanglement of those objects and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime has to be in a superposition of states. \nWhich would mean spacetime has to be quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One particular proposal for this type of \nexperiment has recently been put forward,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "known as a Quantum Gravity-induced Entanglement \nof Masses, QGEM, experiment. I’ll describe the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "original version proposed by Sougato \nBose and collaborators in 2017."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654360,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It uses a Stern-Gerlach interferometer. \nThis is the device that first demonstrated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659800,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the existence of quantum spin back in the \n20s. The original version used silver atoms,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666320,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which have a single unpaired electron in \ntheir outer shell, so the atom takes on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum spin of that lone electron. That \ngives the atom a tiny dipole magnetic field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677560,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This causes the atom to be deflected when it \npasses through the external magnetic field of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Stern-Gerlach device. It’s deflected upwards \nif  its spin axis is point up and downwards  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689600,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if its spin is down. Because quantum spin is \nquantum, all atoms will be measured to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either spin fully up or spin fully down (reflected \nall the way up or reflected all the way down)—"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are no in-betweens for \nquantum properties like this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the atom starts in a superposition state of \nbeing both spin-up and spin-down then it’ll end  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up in a superposition state of being physically \nup or down just before it hits the detector  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719560,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "screen. So we've turned a spin superposition \ninto a spatial superposition. At the screen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726560,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the superposition collapses \ninto one or the other location."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730600,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can also remove the screen and \nuse more magnets to bring the spatial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734840,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superpositions back together, in which \ncase we turn the spatial superposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739360,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back into a spin superposition. Now \nif we measure that spin we should find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it collapses completely randomly \ninto 50-50 spin up or spin down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750640,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we were to run a second Stern-Gerlach \ninterferometer next to the first, the spins  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756120,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d measure at the end of the second device \nwould have no relationship to the spins measured  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761080,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the end of the first. Atoms in the different \ndevices are uncorrelated—they are not entangled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now let’s move the devices really, \nreally close together. The spin-up path  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of interferometer one is right alongside \nthe spin-down path of interferometer two.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s pretend for a moment that the silver \natoms can interact gravitationally. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spin-up-one and spin-down-two atoms \ntug on each other ever so slightly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790240,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the other paths are too far for the atoms \nto interact. Remember that the spin measurements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795680,
    "dDurationMs": 9400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the output of interferometer one versus two \nused to be random—uncorrelated. Up-up, down-down,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805080,
    "dDurationMs": 11640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up-down, down-up should all be equally likely. Any \nquantum interaction should generate entanglement,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818240,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so now, if gravity is quantum, the outputs \nof the two interferometers are entangled and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824840,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "correlated. For example, depending on how this is \nset up you may find that that the interferometers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce opposite spin results to each other very \nvery slightly more often than same spin results."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now single atoms are way too light to produce \na measurable entanglement. But what if we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could do this with many atoms? Specifically, \nwith a nanodiamond. These are tiny diamonds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with widths measured in nanometers. A little \ndefect inside the diamond crystal structure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be created by removing one carbon atom and \nreplacing it with a neighbouring nitrogen atom.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leaves an unpaired electron which \ngives the nanodiamond a net spin. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same technology is actually a candidate \nfor qubit storage in quantum computers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we create nanodiamonds and put their \nunpaired electrons in a spin superposition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878360,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then shoot them through the pair of Stern-Gerlach \ndevices. In principle we should get a spatial  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884720,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "superposition of these relatively massive \ncrystals, and if gravity is quantum, we should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890360,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get entanglement between the devices that will be \nreflected in correlations in their final spins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What would all of this mean if it proved to work?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903320,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well from the point of view of particle \nphysics, the masses get entangled when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907960,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitons are exchanged between the two masses. \nThis is just like in electromagnetism, where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photon exchange leads to the electromagnetic \nforce, and sometimes entanglement. We’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919120,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made a video before on how hard it is to \ndetect single gravitons even in principle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923840,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so this entanglement between the two masses in \nthe QGEM experiment might be considered indirect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence for the existence of gravitons—the \nhypothetical quantum carrier of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934120,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the point of view of general \nrelativity, the superposition of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937520,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational forces could be interpreted as \na superposition of spacetime geometries. So,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime itself would be exhibiting one of \nthe most quantum of all properties. In general,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950640,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a positive QGEM result would be strong \nevidence that gravity is indeed quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are just some examples of experiments that \nhave been proposed—or actually conducted—to test  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963520,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of these ideas about the link between \nquantum mechanics and gravity. Whether to find  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968040,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum effects in the gravitational field, \nor to show that gravity doesn’t even need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971720,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be truly quantum. There are lots of other \nideas and efforts in progress beyond these,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976920,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there’s a good chance that some \nwill bear fruit—perhaps finally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unlocking that century-old mystery \nof the quantum nature of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987000,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS. \nBrilliant is where you learn by doing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with thousands of interactive lessons \nin physics, math, programming, and AI. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996440,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant helps build your critical thinking \nskills through problem solving—not memorizing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002000,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each lesson is filled with hands-on problem \nsolving that let you actually play with concepts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you’re fascinated by AI, “How \nLLMs Work” is an immersive AI workshop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013600,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that lets you to experience and harness the \nmechanics of today's most advanced tools. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017720,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ll Get hands-on with real language models \nas you explore how they build vocabulary, choose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their next word, and more. You’ll Understand \nthe importance of training data by comparing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models trained on Pop lyrics to models trained on \na cookbook, or Big Tech's Terms and Conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035400,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, you’ll get learn how to tune an LLM to \ngenerate different kinds of output—whether it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poetry or a cover letter. To try everything \nBrilliant has to offer for free for a full  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1045480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "30 days, visit brilliant.org/spacetime or click \non the link in the description. If you go now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051880,
    "dDurationMs": 26960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll also get 20% off an \nannual premium subscription."
    } ]
  } ]
}
